"Benjamin knew little of Buddhism, and the comparison is one that a reader makes. The unique existence of a thing in its own time and place that he called aura is close to something Buddhist practice trains a person to notice: this bowl, this breath, as it is now and will not be again. Aura in his sense belongs to the unrepeatable, and the teaching of impermanence says that everything is unrepeatable. On that view the decline of aura is a failure of attention. A printed page has a here and now for anyone who looks at it closely. Benjamin half saw this himself. In his essay on the storyteller he says that a story told aloud bears the marks of the teller as a clay pot bears the handprints of the potter, and he valued such traces wherever he found them.": "b1118643201e",
"Benjamin left no system and hardly any long finished book. His method was the essay, the aphorism, and above all the quotation. He once said that he wished to write a work consisting entirely of quotations, arranged so that they would comment on one another without any connecting argument. The Arcades Project, a thousand pages of notes and excerpts sorted into folders, is the nearest he came. Whether it is a ruin or a new kind of book is still argued over.": "ae93560d86bb",
"Benjamin predicted that reproduction would wear aura away. The opposite seems to have happened. The Mona Lisa became the most famous painting in the world through postcards and posters, and the crowds in front of it are larger than ever. Reproduction works as advertising for the original. Every copy tells its viewer that somewhere there is a real one, and the queue at the museum grows. Film, which Benjamin thought could have no aura, soon produced stars, premieres, and props sold at auction as relics. On this evidence aura is something that people supply to things, and they will supply it to whatever is scarce.": "b9b0583f8787",
"Benjamins best-known essay starts from a plain observation. For most of history a work of art existed in one place. To see it you had to go to it. Printing, and then photography and film, changed this. A photograph of a cathedral can be looked at in a kitchen. Benjamin asked what the copy leaves behind, and answered: the here and now of the original, its unique existence at the place where it is. He called this the aura of the work. Aura depends on distance and on history. The object has come down through time, has been cared for or neglected, and has belonged to rituals and to owners. A reproduction is cut loose from all of that. It meets the viewer wherever the viewer happens to be.": "88325976e75d",
"Frankfurt School": "968b724da182",
"He did not simply mourn the change. Art, he argued, began in the service of ritual, first magical and then religious, and the reverence people feel before an original is a remnant of that. Reproduction frees art from this role. A film has no original, is made to be shown everywhere, and is watched by a crowd that judges it. Benjamin hoped that such art would serve the many, and he saw that it could equally serve those who wished to rule them. Fascism, he wrote, turns politics into a spectacle. The essay does not settle whether the loss of aura is good. It gave later writers the terms in which the question is still discussed.": "8d7cede4b3fe",
"He studied philosophy and literature at several universities, took a doctorate at Bern in 1919, and hoped for a professorship. To qualify he wrote a difficult study of German baroque drama. In 1925 the faculty at Frankfurt let him know that it would be rejected, and he withdrew it. One examiner said that he had not understood a word. After that Benjamin belonged to no institution. He supported himself by writing reviews and essays for newspapers and by giving talks on the radio, many of them for children.": "0a218c9046eb",
"He was drawn in two directions that his friends thought could not be combined. Gershom Scholem, the historian of Jewish mysticism, wanted him to learn Hebrew and come to Jerusalem. Bertolt Brecht and a Latvian actress he loved, Asja Lācis, drew him toward Marxism. He took something from each side and never chose between them. In 1921 he bought a small picture by Paul Klee, Angelus Novus, and kept it with him wherever he lived. In his last essay it becomes the angel of history, who is blown backward into the future with his face turned to the wreckage of the past.": "d4e345be9a6c",
"His friend Adorno made a second objection in letters at the time. Benjamin had been too hopeful about the audience. A crowd watching a film is not thereby made critical. It may be entertained into passivity, and an industry that manufactures entertainment has every reason to keep it so. Benjamins reply was incomplete when he died. Readers today tend to think that each was half right. Reproduction did put art in everyones hands, and what was done with it was mostly sold to them.": "5c687f0a6790",
"The difficulty for readers is that his writing joins things that usually exclude each other: Jewish messianic thought and historical materialism, close attention to texts and large claims about history. Scholem thought the Marxism a mistake that obscured his real gifts. Brecht thought the mysticism the mistake. Arendt said that he was neither a philosopher nor a critic in the usual sense and compared him to a pearl diver, who brings up from the past what has been transformed by long lying on the sea floor. It may be the best description of what he did.": "906d144a0389",
"The two part company over what to do with the feeling. Benjamin treasured the particular and feared its loss. Much of his work is an attempt to rescue things, a childhood, a city, the hopes of the defeated, from being forgotten. The Buddha taught that clinging to what passes is the source of sorrow, and that one may see a thing fully, care for it, and still let it go. Benjamins angel of history wants to stay and mend what has been broken, and cannot. A Buddhist reader may think that the image says more than its author intended. The wish to hold the past in place is itself the storm (Dharma and Western Philosophy D3).": "e1921cb22af1",
"They reached the Spanish border town of Portbou on September 25. The police told them that the rules had changed that day and that they would be sent back to France in the morning. That night Benjamin took an overdose of morphine. He was forty-eight. The next day the others in the group were allowed to travel on. The manuscript in the briefcase was never found. Hannah Arendt, who crossed the same border a few months later, carried a copy of his last essay to New York, and it was through her and through Adorno that his work reached readers after the war.": "8fca7c6d4b4f",
"Walter Benjamin was born in Berlin in 1892, the eldest child of a prosperous Jewish family. His father dealt in antiques and art. He later wrote a book of short pieces about his childhood in which the telephone in the hallway, a sewing box, and the corners of the apartment are described with the attention that other writers give to great events. The habit of finding large meanings in small and discarded things stayed with him.": "5bfe42914194",
"When Hitler came to power in 1933, Benjamin left Germany for good and settled in Paris. He was poor, often ill, and dependent on a stipend from the exiled Institute for Social Research. He spent his days in the national library gathering thousands of quotations for a book on the shopping arcades of nineteenth-century Paris, through which he hoped to show how a whole society dreams. The book was never finished. In those years he also wrote the essays for which he is now read, on the storyteller, on Kafka, on Baudelaire, and on the work of art in the age of its reproduction.": "cd2ace435c54",
"When war began in 1939 the French interned him for some months as an enemy alien. In June 1940, as the German army approached Paris, he fled south. Friends in America had obtained an entry visa for him, but he could not get permission to leave France. In late September he joined a small group that crossed the Pyrenees on foot by a smugglers path. He had a weak heart and carried a heavy briefcase containing a manuscript that he said was more important than his life.": "caa6465597c9",
